23056694-58b5-407a-aeb7-08a666314f43|Myanmar: Staff Report for the 2018 Article IV Consultation—Debt Sustainability Analysis|18|text|2. The new LIC DSF determines the debt sustainability thresholds by calculating a composite indicator (CI). In the previous debt sustainability framework (DSF), debt-carrying capacity was determined by the World Bank's CPIA score. Under this methodology, Myanmar was classified as having weak policy performance with a Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA) average of 3.07 for the period 2014-16. The CI, however, is based on a weighted average of several factors such as the country's real GDP growth, remittances, international reserves, and world growth and the CPIA score. $ ^{2} $ The calculation of the CI is based on 10-year averages of the variables, across 5 years of historical data and 5 years of projection, and the corresponding CPIA. For Myanmar, the CI score based on both the 2018 April WEO and 2018 October WEO data corresponds to a medium rating and thus the final debt carrying capacity classification for this DSA is medium. Thus, compared with the previous DSF methodology, the PV of debt-to-exports threshold has now increased from 100 percent to 180 percent while the debt service-to-exports and debt service-to-revenue thresholds remain unchanged. The indicative threshold for the PV of total public debt-to-GDP has been increased from 38 to 55 percent of GDP. A summary of the thresholds used in the exercise are included in the table below.
d775137f-bb5d-42ee-8a2a-d0d0bc4aaa55|Guyana: Staff Report for the 2019 Article IV Consultation—Debt Sustainability Analysis|57|text|11. Guyana is assessed to have a "medium" debt carrying capacity.Based on the April 2019 WEO macroeconomic framework, the country's composite indicator (CI) score is 3.01, within the range of 2.69-3.05 for "medium" rated countries.From the 2018 Guidance Note on The Bank-Fund Debt Sustainability Framework for Low Income Countries,the relevant indicative thresholds for public and publicly guaranteed (PPG) external debt in this category are:40 percent for the PV of debt-to-GDP ratio,180 percent for the PV of debt-to-exports ratio,15 percent for the debt service-to-exports ratio,and 18 percent for the debt service-to-revenue ratio.The benchmark of the PV of total public debt for "medium" debt carrying capacity is 55 percent.
d775137f-bb5d-42ee-8a2a-d0d0bc4aaa55|Guyana: Staff Report for the 2019 Article IV Consultation—Debt Sustainability Analysis|59|table|¦ Components ¦ Coefficients(A) ¦ 10-year average values(B) ¦ CI Score components(A*B)= (C) ¦ Contribution of components ¦ ¦ --- ¦ --- ¦ --- ¦ --- ¦ --- ¦ ¦ CPIA ¦ 0.39 ¦ 3.31 ¦ 1.28 ¦ 0.42 ¦ ¦ Real growth rate(in percent) ¦ 2.72 ¦ 7.82 ¦ 0.21 ¦ 0.07 ¦ ¦ Import coverage of reserves(in percent) ¦ 4.05 ¦ 30.90 ¦ 1.25 ¦ 0.42 ¦ ¦ Import coverage of reserves2(in percent) ¦ -3.99 ¦ 9.55 ¦ -0.38 ¦ -0.13 ¦ ¦ Remittances(in percent) ¦ 2.02 ¦ 8.47 ¦ 0.17 ¦ 0.06 ¦ ¦ World economic growth(in percent) ¦ 13.52 ¦ 3.56 ¦ 0.48 ¦ 0.16 ¦ ¦  ¦  ¦  ¦ 3.01 ¦ 100% ¦ ¦ CI Score ¦  ¦  ¦  ¦  ¦ ¦  ¦  ¦  ¦ Medium ¦  ¦ ¦ CI rating ¦  ¦  ¦ Medium ¦  ¦
54709494-c9b9-4f69-b2f1-47283b24afa6|Republic of Moldova: Staff Report for the 2025 Article IV Consultation—Debt Sustainability Analysis|8|footnote|$^{2}$ Moldova's Composite Indicator (CI) index, based on the October 2025 WEO update and the World Bank 2024 Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA), indicates that the debt carrying capacity remains strong (3.055), but close to the lower bound of 3.05 of strong debt carrying capacity.
88470724-840d-4218-ae9e-5b9447c117ed|Nepal: Staff Report for the 2026 Article IV Consultation and Seventh Review Under the Extended Credit Facility|7|footnote|$^{1}$ Nepal's debt carrying capacity remains strong, based on Nepal's composite indicator (CI) score. The CI is calculated at 3.14, based on the October 2025 World Economic Outlook (WEO) and the 2024 World Bank Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA) index.
88470724-840d-4218-ae9e-5b9447c117ed|Nepal: Staff Report for the 2026 Article IV Consultation and Seventh Review Under the Extended Credit Facility|83|text|20. Nepal's debt carrying capacity is strong. A composite indicator (CI) is used to capture the different factors affecting a country's debt carrying capacity. The CI is a weighted average of the World Bank's Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA) score, the country's real GDP growth, remittances, foreign exchange reserves, and global growth. The calculation of the CI is based on 10-year averages of the variables, across 5 years of historical data and 5 years of projections. Nepal's CI score is calculated at 3.14, based on the October 2025 World Economic Outlook and the 2024 World Bank CPIA index, which reflects a strong Debt carrying capacity (Text Table 5). This classification determines the applicable external and public debt thresholds used in the risk rating assessment. For countries with strong capacity, the thresholds are: (i) PV of external debt at 55 percent of GDP and 240 percent of exports; (ii) external debt service at 21 percent of exports and 23 percent of revenue; and (iii) PV of total public debt at 70 percent of GDP.
88470724-840d-4218-ae9e-5b9447c117ed|Nepal: Staff Report for the 2026 Article IV Consultation and Seventh Review Under the Extended Credit Facility|87|image|This figure presents Text Table 5, titled "Nepal: Debt Carrying Capacity and Thresholds," containing four distinct data sections. The top-left section identifies the country as Nepal (Code 558) with a Debt Carrying Capacity classification of "Strong." A sub-table shows this "Strong" classification is consistent across the final result, current vintage, previous vintage, and two previous vintages, all associated with a value of 3.14.  The top-right section lists applicable thresholds. Under "EXTERNAL debt burden thresholds," the PV of debt in percent of Exports is 240, GDP is 55, Debt service in percent of Revenue is 21, and there is an unlabelled row with the value 23. A separate box for "TOTAL public debt benchmark" lists the PV of total public debt in percent of GDP as 70.  The bottom-left section details the "Calculation of the CI Index." It lists five components with their Coefficients (A), 10-year average values (B), calculated CI Score components (C), and percentage contribution. CPIA Real growth rate has a coefficient of 0.39 and contributes 42%. Import coverage of reserves has a coefficient of 2.72 and contributes 3%. Import coverage of reserves squared has a coefficient of 4.05 and contributes 74%. Remittances has a coefficient of -3.99 and contributes -42%. World economic growth has a coefficient of 2.02 and contributes 10%. The table sums these to a total CI Score of 3.14 (100% contribution) and a CI rating of Strong. Note: The visual alignment in the image places coefficients and values slightly differently than standard reading order suggests; specifically, the coefficient 13.52 and value 3.04 appear aligned with World economic growth, while 2.02 and 15.49 align with Remittances, and -3.99 and 32.84 align with Import coverage of reserves^2.  The bottom-right section, labeled "New framework," defines cut-off values for the CI index. A Weak rating corresponds to CI < 2.69. A Medium rating corresponds to 2.69 ≤ CI ≤ 3.05. A Strong rating corresponds to CI > 3.05.
