35cea89f-f18f-4918-881c-39314e7ea063|Ghana: Staff Report for the 2026 Article IV Consultation, Sixth Review Under the Arrangement Under the Extende|5|table|¦ Risk of external debt distress ¦ Moderate ¦ ¦ --- ¦ --- ¦ ¦ Overall risk of debt distress ¦ Moderate ¦ ¦ Granularity in the risk rating ¦ Limited space to absorb shocks ¦ ¦ Application of judgment ¦ No ¦ ¦ $^{1}$ This DSA is prepared in line with the Guidance Note of the Joint Bank-Fund Debt Sustainability Framework for Low Income Countries, February 2018 and with its 2024 Supplement.$^{2}$ The Composite Indicator (CI) score of 2.98 remains between the cut-off values for weak and strong debt-carrying capacity, 2.69 and 3.05, respectively.The CI is the weighted average of the 10-year average of the World Bank's Country Policy and Institutional Assessment(CPIA)score and macroeconomic variables from the April 2026 WEO vintage. ¦ $^{1}$ This DSA is prepared in line with the Guidance Note of the Joint Bank-Fund Debt Sustainability Framework for Low Income Countries, February 2018 and with its 2024 Supplement.$^{2}$ The Composite Indicator (CI) score of 2.98 remains between the cut-off values for weak and strong debt-carrying capacity, 2.69 and 3.05, respectively.The CI is the weighted average of the 10-year average of the World Bank's Country Policy and Institutional Assessment(CPIA)score and macroeconomic variables from the April 2026 WEO vintage. ¦
35cea89f-f18f-4918-881c-39314e7ea063|Ghana: Staff Report for the 2026 Article IV Consultation, Sixth Review Under the Arrangement Under the Extende|98|text|27. Ghana's debt carrying capacity is assessed as "medium", unchanged from the last DSA (Text Table 6). The Composite Indicator (CI) score of 2.98 remains between the cut-off values for weak and strong debt-carrying capacity, 2.69 and 3.05, respectively, suggesting a medium debt carrying capacity. The CI is the weighted average of the 10-year average of the World Bank's Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA) score and macro-economic variables from the April 2026 WEO vintage.
35cea89f-f18f-4918-881c-39314e7ea063|Ghana: Staff Report for the 2026 Article IV Consultation, Sixth Review Under the Arrangement Under the Extende|111|image|The image displays Text Table 6, titled "Ghana: Debt Carrying Capacity," sourced from CI score calculations based on the LIC DSF. The top section indicates a final Debt Carrying Capacity classification of "Medium." A small table compares this final classification with classifications based on the current vintage (Medium, 2.98), the previous vintage (Medium, 2.83), and the two previous vintages (Medium, 2.84). To the right, two dark blue boxes labeled "APPLICABLE" display thresholds. The first lists External debt burden thresholds: PV of debt in % of Exports at 180, GDP at 40, Debt service in % of Exports at 15, and Revenue at 18. The second lists the Total public debt benchmark as PV of total public debt in percent of GDP at 55.  The middle section, titled "Calculation of the CI Index," presents a table breaking down the index components. It lists Components, Coefficients (A), 10-year average values (B), CI Score components (A*B = C), and Contribution of components. The components and their respective data are: CPIA (Coeff 0.385, Value 3.437, Score 1.32, Contrib 44%); Real growth rate (Coeff 2.719, Value 4.850, Score 0.13, Contrib 4%); Import coverage of reserves (Coeff 4.052, Value 40.552, Score 1.64, Contrib 55%); Import coverage of reserves^2 (Coeff -3.990, Value 16.445, Score -0.66, Contrib -22%); Remittances (Coeff 2.022, Value 2.491, Score 0.05, Contrib 2%); and World economic growth (Coeff 13.520, Value 3.637, Score 0.49, Contrib 16%). The bottom rows show a total CI Score of 2.984 (100%) and a CI rating of Medium. Adjacent to this is a "New framework" table showing Cut-off values: Weak for CI < 2.69, Medium for 2.69 ≤ CI ≤ 3.05, and Strong for CI > 3.05.  The bottom section, titled "Reference: Thresholds by Classiciation," contains two tables. The left table lists External debt burden thresholds for Weak, Medium, and Strong classifications. For PV of debt in % of Exports, values are 140, 180, and 240; for GDP, 30, 40, and 55. For Debt service in % of Exports, values are 10, 15, and 21; for Revenue, 14, 18, and 23. The right table lists the Total public debt benchmark (PV of total public debt in percent of GDP) for Weak (35), Medium (55), and Strong (70).
01b8e32f-277b-4e14-8391-e7bb5ea5181c|Kenya: Staff Report for 2021 Article IV Consultation, Second Reviews Under the Extended Arrangement Under the |7|footnote|$^{1}$ The DSA analysis reflects a debt carrying capacity of Medium considering Kenya's Composite Indicator Index of 3.04, based on the IMF's 2021 October World Economic Outlook and the 2020 World Bank Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA).
01b8e32f-277b-4e14-8391-e7bb5ea5181c|Kenya: Staff Report for 2021 Article IV Consultation, Second Reviews Under the Extended Arrangement Under the |77|text|17. Kenya's debt carrying capacity is assessed as Medium (Text Table 6). The debt carrying capacity determines the applicable thresholds for the PPG external and total public debt sustainability indicators used in the assessment. $^{17}$ It is informed by the value for Kenya of the Composite Indicator (CI) Index of 3.04, $^{18}$ which incorporates data from the IMF's 2021 October World Economic Outlook (WEO) macroeconomic projections and the 2020 World Bank's Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA). The previous value of the CI Index, based on the IMF's April 2021 WEO, exceeded the threshold for a Strong rating. According to the DSA methodology, reverting to the pre-pandemic assessment of Kenya's debt-carrying capacity of Strong would have required a second consecutive Strong signal based on the published October 2021 WEO forecast vintage. Results from the latter show, however, that the CI Index falls again below the 3.05 threshold, and hence will not trigger an upward revision of the assessment from the current rating of Medium. $^{19}$
01b8e32f-277b-4e14-8391-e7bb5ea5181c|Kenya: Staff Report for 2021 Article IV Consultation, Second Reviews Under the Extended Arrangement Under the |87|image|The figure presents a data table calculating a Country Indicator (CI) Score and applicable debt thresholds. The main table contains five columns: Components, Coefficients (A), 10-year average values (B), CI Score components (A*B) = (C), and Contribution of components.  The rows list the following components with their respective values: - CPIA: Coefficient 0.385, 10-year average 3.70, Score component 1.43, Contribution 47%. - Real growth rate (in percent): Coefficient 2.719, 10-year average 4.67, Score component 0.13, Contribution 4%. - Import coverage of reserves (in percent): Coefficient 4.052, 10-year average 42.67, Score component 1.73, Contribution 57%. - Import coverage of reserves^2 (in percent): Coefficient -3.990, 10-year average 18.21, Score component -0.73, Contribution -24%. - Remittances (in percent): Coefficient 2.022, 10-year average 3.04, Score component 0.06, Contribution 2%. - World economic growth (in percent): Coefficient 13.520, 10-year average 3.14, Score component 0.42, Contribution 14%.  Below these rows, the total CI Score is listed as 3.04, representing 100% contribution. The corresponding CI rating is labeled as Medium.  Beneath the main calculation table is a section titled "Applicable thresholds" containing two smaller tables. The left table, under the header APPLICABLE and subheader EXTERNAL debt burden thresholds, lists specific percentage benchmarks: PV of debt in % of Exports is 180, PV of debt in % of GDP is 40, Debt service in % of Exports is 15, and Debt service in % of Revenue is 18. The right table, under the header APPLICABLE and subheader TOTAL public debt benchmark, shows the PV of total public debt in percent of GDP as 55.
