55fdb5d7-48fc-4767-a0a1-242c26c4c2a2|Cabo Verde: Staff Report for the 2019 Article IV Consultation and Request for a Policy Coordination Instrument|62|text|12. Cabo Verde's debt-carrying capacity is assessed as "strong" as in the 2018 Article IV DSA (Text Table 5). In the previous framework, the debt-carrying capacity was solely informed by the World Bank Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA) score. The new methodology relies on a composite indicator (CI) combining the CPIA score, external conditions captured by world economic growth and country-specific factors. Based on data from the April 2019 World Economic Outlook (WEO) vintage and the 2017 CPIA, the CI score for Cabo Verde stands at 3.28, which is above the threshold of 3.05 applicable for a "strong" rating. The CI score reflects positive contributions from the CPIA (44 percent), international reserves (31 percent), world growth (15 percent), remittances (6 percent) and country real growth rate (3 percent). Debt burden thresholds implied by the strong debt-carrying capacity under the previous and new frameworks are summarized in Text Table 6.
55fdb5d7-48fc-4767-a0a1-242c26c4c2a2|Cabo Verde: Staff Report for the 2019 Article IV Consultation and Request for a Policy Coordination Instrument|64|table|¦ Components ¦ Coefficients(A) ¦ 10-year average values(B) ¦ CI Score components(A*B)= (C) ¦ Contribution of components ¦ ¦ --- ¦ --- ¦ --- ¦ --- ¦ --- ¦ ¦ CPIA ¦ 0.385 ¦ 3.783 ¦ 1.45 ¦ 44% ¦ ¦ Real growth rate(in percent) ¦ 2.719 ¦ 4.010 ¦ 0.11 ¦ 3% ¦ ¦ Import coverage of reserves(in percent) ¦ 4.052 ¦ 48.773 ¦ 1.98 ¦ 60% ¦ ¦ Import coverage of reserves^2(in percent) ¦ -3.990 ¦ 23.788 ¦ -0.95 ¦ -29% ¦ ¦ Remittances(in percent) ¦ 2.022 ¦ 10.179 ¦ 0.21 ¦ 6% ¦ ¦ World economic growth(in percent) ¦ 13.520 ¦ 3.559 ¦ 0.48 ¦ 15% ¦ ¦ CI Score ¦  ¦  ¦ 3.28 ¦ 100% ¦ ¦ CI rating ¦  ¦  ¦ Strong ¦  ¦
2285d165-9a4b-4c45-acdc-dc8b773d9ee8|Cabo Verde: Staff Report for the 2025 Article IV Consultation, Seventh Review Under the Extended Credit Facili|11|footnote|$^{1}$ Cabo Verde' debt carrying capacity is assessed as strong, given a Composite Indicator of 3.30 based on October 2025 WEO projections and the 2024 Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA).
2285d165-9a4b-4c45-acdc-dc8b773d9ee8|Cabo Verde: Staff Report for the 2025 Article IV Consultation, Seventh Review Under the Extended Credit Facili|81|text|(Text Table 4). The debt-carrying capacity is determined by the composite indicator (CI) combining the World Bank Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA), external conditions captured by world economic growth and country-specific factors. The methodology is based on the data from the October 2025 World Economic Outlook (WEO) vintage and the 2024 CPIA, the CI score for Cabo Verde stands at 3.3, which is slightly above the last DSA and the threshold of 3.05 applicable for a "strong" rating. The CI score reflects positive contributions from the CPIA (46 percent), international reserves (33 percent), world growth (12 percent), remittances (6 percent) and country's real growth rate (4 percent) that are all broadly in line with the last DSA. Debt burden thresholds associated with the strong debt carrying capacity under the framework are summarized in Text Table 5.
86c97912-8b2e-46e3-8726-3d8a760eb96f|Cameroon: Staff Report for the 2023 Article IV Consultation, Fifth Reviews Under the Extended Credit Facility |89|text|21. Cameroon's debt carrying capacity remains weak.The Composite Indicator (CI) score based on the October 2023 WEO projections and the 2022 World Bank CPIA score is 2.61, signaling a weak debtcarrying capacity. $^{13}$ The higher CI score compared to the previous DSA mainly reflects higher reserves, despite lower remittances and world economic growth (Text Table 7).
c5faa177-de31-466e-b5d4-341c631343dd|Kingdom Of Lesotho: Staff Report for the 2019 Article IV Consultation|322|image|The image displays Table 4, titled "Lesotho: Debt Carrying Capacity," which presents a financial assessment for the country Lesotho (Country Code 666). The table is divided into several sections detailing thresholds, calculations, and reference data.  In the top left section, "Debt Carrying Capacity and Thresholds," the overall classification for Lesotho is listed as "Medium." A sub-table compares this final classification against previous vintages: the current vintage score is 2.94 (Medium), the previous vintage score was 2.90 (Medium), and the classification based on the two previous vintages was 3.33 (Medium). A note indicates that until the April 2019 WEO vintage is released, scores are based solely on the CPIA per the previous framework.  To the right, under "Applicable thresholds," specific benchmarks for Lesotho are listed. For "EXTERNAL debt burden thresholds," the Present Value (PV) of debt in percent of Exports is set at 180, and in percent of GDP at 40. The Debt service in percent of Exports is 15, and in percent of Revenue is 18. A separate box for "TOTAL public debt benchmark" lists the PV of total public debt in percent of GDP as 55. A red arrow points from the main classification area to these applicable thresholds.  The middle left section, "Calculation of the CI Index," breaks down the mathematical components used to determine the score. It lists six components with their Coefficients (A), 10-year average values (B), resulting CI Score components (A*B = C), and percentage contribution. The components are: - CPIA: Coefficient 0.385, Value 3.375, Score 1.30, Contribution 44% - Real growth rate (in percent): Coefficient 2.719, Value 2.141, Score 0.06, Contribution 2% - Import coverage of reserves (in percent): Coefficient 4.052, Value 37.032, Score 1.50, Contribution 51% - Import coverage of reserves^2 (in percent): Coefficient -3.990, Value 13.714, Score -0.55, Contribution -19% - Remittances (in percent): Coefficient 2.022, Value 7.423, Score 0.15, Contribution 5% - World economic growth (in percent): Coefficient 13.520, Value 3.579, Score 0.48, Contribution 16%  The total CI Score is calculated as 2.94, summing to 100% contribution, resulting in a "Medium" CI rating.  To the right of the calculation table is a "New framework" section showing cut-off values for classifications: Weak is defined as CI < 2.69, Medium is 2.69 ≤ CI ≤ 3.05, and Strong is CI > 3.05.  The bottom section, "Reference: Thresholds by Classiciation," provides a comparative table for external and total debt benchmarks across Weak, Medium, and Strong categories. For EXTERNAL debt burden thresholds, the Medium column lists PV of debt in % of Exports as 180, GDP as 40, Debt service in % of Exports as 15, and Revenue as 18. For the TOTAL public debt benchmark, the Medium column lists PV of total public debt in percent of GDP as 55. The Weak and Strong columns provide lower and higher respective thresholds for comparison.
