494ba491-055f-489f-a839-ef024bbdcaec|Tonga: Staff Report for the 2022 Article IV Consultation and Request for Disbursement Under the Rapid Credit F|39|text|7. Tonga's debt-carrying capacity applied in the 2022 DSA is strong. Tonga's Composite Indicator (CI) index, which has been calculated based on the October 2021 WEO and the 2020 CPIA is 3.12, indicating that the county's debt-carrying capacity is strong according to the revised low-income country (LIC)-DSA framework.
18ea848e-e845-41f4-8ac1-544d4728cf3f|Union of the Comoros: Staff Report for the 2025 Article IV Consultation, Fifth Review Under the Extended Credi|49|text|11. Comoros is assessed to have medium debt carrying capacity, unchanged from the 2024 DSA (Text Table 5), with a Composite Indicator (CI) of 2.85 based on October 2025 WEO projections and the 2024 Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA) rating of 2.7. As a result, this DSA continues to use the same external debt burden thresholds and total public benchmarks for countries with medium debt carrying capacity (Text Table 5). The relevant thresholds indicating high risk are 40 percent for the PV of debt-to-GDP ratio, 180 percent for the PV of debt-to-exports ratio, 15 percent for the debt service-to-exports ratio, and 18 percent for the debt service-to-revenue ratio. These thresholds are applicable to the public and publicly guaranteed external debt. For the PV of total public debt-to-GDP ratio, the benchmark is 55 percent.
