528c0cf3-92c6-4a7e-9575-6e187f646242|Kingdom of Lesotho: Staff Report for the 2023 Article IV Consultation—Debt Sustainability Analysis|6|footnote|$^{1}$ This DSA updated the previous Joint DSA from May 2022 (IMF Country Report No. 22/161). The DSA analysis reflects a debt carrying capacity of Medium considering Lesotho's Composite Indicator Index of 2.86, based on the IMF's April 2023 World Economic Outlook and the 2022 World Bank Country Policy and Institutional Assessment (CPIA).
528c0cf3-92c6-4a7e-9575-6e187f646242|Kingdom of Lesotho: Staff Report for the 2023 Article IV Consultation—Debt Sustainability Analysis|61|text|10. Lesotho has a medium debt carrying capacity (Text Table 5). Debt carrying capacity is determined by a composite indicator (CI) that includes the World Bank's Country Policy and Institutional Assessment score, global economic growth, Lesotho's real growth rate, import coverage of reserves, and remittances. The composite indicator for the April 2023 WEO data and the World Bank's 2021 CPIA score yields a medium CI rating (2.86), similar to the previous vintage.
528c0cf3-92c6-4a7e-9575-6e187f646242|Kingdom of Lesotho: Staff Report for the 2023 Article IV Consultation—Debt Sustainability Analysis|63|image|This figure is Text Table 5, titled "Lesotho: Debt Carrying Capacity and Thresholds." It presents a structured analysis of Lesotho's debt sustainability using the IMF/WorldBank Debt Sustainability Analysis (DSA) framework.  The top left section identifies the country as Lesotho (Code 666) with a final Debt Carrying Capacity classification of "Medium." A breakdown shows this classification is consistent across the current vintage (2.86), previous vintage (2.90), and two previous vintages (2.97).  The top right section lists applicable thresholds. External debt burden thresholds are set at a Present Value (PV) of debt equal to 180% of exports, 40% of GDP, and debt service equal to 15% of exports and 18% of revenue. The Total public debt benchmark is a PV of total public debt at 55% of GDP.  The middle left section details the "Calculation of the CI Index." It lists components including CPIA (coefficient 0.385, score 1.27, contribution 44%), Real growth rate (contribution 0%), Import coverage of reserves (coefficient 4.052, score 1.28, contribution 45%), Import coverage of reserves squared (negative contribution -14%), Remittances (contribution 11%), and World economic growth (contribution 14%). The total CI Score is calculated as 2.863, resulting in a "Medium" CI rating.  The middle right section outlines the "New framework" cut-off values for the CI Index: Weak is defined as CI < 2.69, Medium as 2.69 ≤ CI ≤ 3.05, and Strong as CI > 3.05.  The bottom section, "Reference: Thresholds by Classification," provides a comparative table. For External debt burden thresholds, Weak values are PV of debt at 140% of exports and 30% of GDP, and debt service at 10% of exports and 14% of revenue. Medium values (matching Lesotho's applicable thresholds) are 180% of exports, 40% of GDP, 15% of exports, and 18% of revenue. Strong values are 240% of exports, 55% of GDP, 21% of exports, and 23% of revenue. For the Total public debt benchmark, the PV of total public debt in percent of GDP is listed as 35 for Weak, 55 for Medium, and 70 for Strong.
